#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 540.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143686} :activity/id #uuid "49acebb3-e5bb-53c3-a854-dacc58b65725" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050610 :topic/id #uuid "589205b6-43f1-59a1-a894-6d8e8438cfcd" :topic/math-academy-id 2381} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143686 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186144084 :tutorial/content "Suppose we want to multiply the following mixed numbers:\n\n$$\n1\\,\\dfrac 1 {3} \\times 1\\,\\dfrac 2{5}\n$$\n\nTo solve this problem, we can use the following three steps:\n\n**Step 1:** Convert both mixed numbers to improper fractions. \n\n- First, we write $1\\,\\dfrac 1 {3}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n1\\,\\dfrac 1 {3}=\\dfrac{(3\\times 1)+1}{3}= \\dfrac43\n$$\n\n- We also write $1\\,\\dfrac25$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n1\\,\\dfrac25=\\dfrac{(5\\times 1)+2}{5}= \\dfrac75\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Multiply the fractions by multiplying the numerators and denominators separately:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac43\\times\\dfrac75=  \\dfrac {4\\times 7} {3\\times 5} = \\dfrac{28}{15}\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Simplify or convert back to a mixed number, if necessary:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{28}{15} = 1 \\, \\text{R} 13 = 1 \\, \\dfrac{13}{15}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the answer is $1 \\, \\dfrac{13}{15}.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "0435613c-64c1-5d4e-88c5-4feb68728088" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8628 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "1819f7b8-f4b6-511f-bd23-27cecff8194c" :step/math-academy-id 136323 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143687}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143687 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142809 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142620 :question/id #uuid "68ab3d23-d9c7-5037-9ff5-1803bee39a02" :question/math-academy-id "e-13826"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f481bacc-75b7-525d-8784-9e5138117597" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050115} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050927}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143274} {:d